You can achieve sorting the numbers in ascending order by using the bubble sort algorithm. Here's an example solution:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int arr[] = {65, 69, 28, 16, 99, 105};
    int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);
    int i, j, temp;

    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) { 
        for (j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) { 
                temp = arr[j]; 
                arr[j] = arr[j+1]; 
                arr[j+1] = temp; 
            }
        }
    }

    printf("Sorted array: \n"); 
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%d ", arr[i]); 
    }
    printf("\n"); 

    return 0;
}
```

In this solution, we are using a nested loop to repeatedly compare adjacent elements of the array and swap them if they are in the wrong order. The outer loop runs from 0 to n-1, and the inner loop runs from 0 to n-i-1, where i is the current iteration of the outer loop. This is because in each iteration of the outer loop, the largest element in the unsorted part of the array is bubbled up to the end. So, in subsequent iterations, we don't need to compare and swap it again.

The `temp` variable is used as a temporary variable to hold the value of an element during swapping.

After sorting the array, we print the sorted array using a loop to iterate over each element and `printf` function.

Finally, the program outputs the sorted array.

